// Examples verified on morloc 0.110.1 dev (2026-10-26): each program was built
// and run (compiler test-suite goldens spec-cost-16-1 and spec-cost-4-1), and
// each listing is the output of `morloc typecheck --realize`.

[[placement-costs]]
=== Choosing where calls run

When a function has implementations in more than one language, for example
the same name sourced from both a Python file and a {cpp} header
(<<term-polymorphism>> covers this in full), the compiler chooses one of them
for every call. It chooses the placement that crosses between languages the
fewest times, counting first the crossings in code that may run many times
per call. This section explains
that rule and how to see the placement it picks.

You do not need any of this to write a working program, and there is nothing
to annotate: the placement depends only on your code.

==== The rule

The compiler compares placements by these, in order:

. *Crossings, deepest first.* A crossing is a call from one pool into
  another, about 20 us whatever it carries. A function you pass to a
  sourced function, such as the step of a `map` or a `fold`, may be called
  many times per call, so its crossings count one level deeper than the
  call; so do the crossings in the body of a recursive function. One
  crossing at a deeper level outweighs any number at a shallower one.
. *Language preference.* Among placements that cross equally often, {cpp}
  comes first, then Rust, then Python, then R.
. *Size.* A sourced function beats a definition in Morloc that does the same
  work by calling several.

Two sources of one function in one language are an error, since nothing
could choose between them; import only one of them.

[[placement-listing]]
==== Seeing the placement

`morloc typecheck --realize` prints the placement of every exported function
before the generated code. Each line is a named call and the language its
implementation runs in, with what it calls indented beneath it. A `*` marks a
line in a different language from the line above it: a crossing. The listing
shows the program as it is built: a function defined in Morloc appears
through the calls in its body, except a recursive one, which keeps its own
line.

Here `twice` has a Python and a {cpp} implementation:

[source, morloc]
----
module main (doubled, shown)

import root-py
import root-cpp

source Py from "f.py" ("twice", "show_int" as showInt)
source Cpp from "f.hpp" ("twice")

twice :: Int -> Int
showInt :: Int -> Str

doubled :: Int -> Int
doubled x = twice x

shown :: Int -> Str
shown x = showInt (twice x)
----

[source, console]
----
$ morloc typecheck --realize main.loc
placement:
  doubled [cpp]
    twice [cpp]
  shown [py]
    showInt [py]
      twice [py]
----

`doubled` has no other calls, so nothing crosses either way and {cpp} wins
by preference. In `shown`, moving `twice` to {cpp} would cross twice, so it
stays in Python beside `showInt`.

The next program maps a Python-only function over a list made in {cpp} and
sums the result in {cpp}; `mapEach` exists in both languages:

[source, morloc]
----
total :: Int -> Int
total n = totalOf (mapEach step (upto n))
----

[source, console]
----
$ morloc typecheck --realize main.loc
placement:
  total [cpp]
    totalOf [cpp]
      mapEach [py] *
        step [py]
        upto [cpp] *
----

Mapping in {cpp} would cross into Python once per element. Mapping in Python
crosses twice in all, the list in and the result out, so `mapEach` runs in
Python.

